SUMMARY:Astronomy & Astrophysics Research Day
DESCRIPTION:The NC Museum of Natural Sciences is a research-based museum with Museum staff who conduct novel research and steward our collections. Our Research Highlight Days feature these researchers and their fields of study. \nThis event will be held on the first and third floors of the Nature Research Center. \nJoin us as we celebrate Astronomy and Astrophysics Research at this event! \n\nPresentations\n11 AM Observing Massive Stars in the Galaxy\nDr. Rachel Smith\, NC Museum of Natural Sciences \nDr. Rachel Smith will present her current research on the formation of planetary systems in the Galaxy. Highlights will be recent observations of massive young stars\, including a unique “runaway” star\, obtained with the NASA Infrared Telescope Facility in Hawaii. \nRachel Smith is an observational astronomer who is interested in how planetary systems form and evolve. She focuses on the chemical pathways that lead to Earth-like planets and the molecules needed for life. Rachel uses large ground-based observatories to gather spectra from stars in giant molecular clouds — nearby factories for new stars and planets. Rachel is especially interested in how data from young stars connect to the formation of our own solar system about 4.6 billion years ago.\nRachel is Head the Astronomy & Astrophysics Research Lab and Curator of Meteorites at the NC Museum of Natural Sciences. She is also a Professor in the Dept. of Physics & Astronomy at Appalachian State University\, and is an Adjunct Professor at UNC-Chapel Hill. \n12 PM Making Sense of Spiral Galaxies: How Volunteers and AI Team Up to Identify Individual Spiral Arms\nDr. Patrick Treuthardt\, NC Museum of Natural Sciences \nThe winding arms of spiral galaxies are one of their most obvious features. By measuring how tightly the arms are wrapped\, we can reveal clues about other components that are much harder to observe\, such as dark matter content and central black hole mass. By partnering volunteers with AI\, we aim to identify individual spiral arms in tens of thousands of galaxies\, measure how tightly each arm is wound\, and use those measurements to estimate these elusive properties. Doing so will help us better understand how galaxies similar to our own Milky Way form and evolve. \nDr. Patrick Treuthardt is the Assistant Head of the Astronomy & Astrophysics Research Lab at the North Carolina Museum of Natural Sciences and a Research Affiliate in the Department of Computing Sciences at Coastal Carolina University. He studies the shapes and structures of nearby galaxies as well as the underlying physics that produces them. He is also the lead researcher of the Spiral Graph citizen science project and its follow-up\, Cluster Buster. \n\nExhibits and Activities\nSpiral Graph: Cluster Buster\nWe will demonstrate and discuss Spiral Graph: Cluster Buster\, our online citizen science project dedicated to identifying individual arms in spiral galaxies and measuring their winding. The degree of winding serves as a proxy for properties such as the mass of the dark matter halo and the mass of the black hole found in a galaxy’s nucleus. Our goal is to find galaxies that will help us understand the co-formation and co-evolution of spiral galaxies and their central supermassive black holes. \nRaleigh Astronomy Club\nRAC will have a table with astronomy related handouts and will answer questions about astronomy and telescopes. \nDiscovering New Worlds: The Science of Exploring Space\nNASA/JPL Solar System Ambassadors\nFrom distant exoplanets far beyond our solar system to galaxies at the edge of the observable universe\, scientists are constantly uncovering new worlds and new mysteries. Meet a NASA/JPL Solar System Ambassador and learn how astronomers use observations\, technology\, and scientific research to explore our universe. \nAstrophysics Research Highlights\nThis table will showcase new student research by undergraduates in Physics & Astronomy at Appalachian State University.\n3rd Floor \nVisitors from Space: Meteorites from Asteroids\, the Moon\, and Mars\nWe will showcase several meteorites from the Museum collection\, including new samples from the asteroid belt\, the Moon\, and Mars.\n3rd Floor
URL:https://www.naturalsciences.org/calendar/event/astronomy-astrophysics-research-day/
LOCATION:Downtown Raleigh\, 11 West Jones Street\, Raleigh\, NC\, 27601\, United States
CATEGORIES:Adults,All Ages,Educators,Special Events
